      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PRIMARY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To characterize an acute stress reaction (ASR) following an improvised explosive device (IED) blast-related mild traumatic brain injury (mTBI)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "RESEARCH DESIGN",
          "content": "Participants were male, US military personnel treated in Afghanistan within 4 days following an IED-related mTBI event (n\u2009=\u2009239)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS AND PROCEDURES",
          "content": "Demographics, diagnosis of ASR, injury history and self-reported mTBIs, blast exposures and psychological health histories were recorded."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOMES AND RESULTS",
          "content": "In total, 12.5% of patients met ASR criteria. Patients with ASR were significantly younger and junior in rank (p\u2009<\u20090.05). Patients with ASR were more likely to experience the IED-blast while dismounted, report a loss of consciousness (LOC) and higher pain levels (p\u2009<\u20090.05). Adjusting for age and rank, multivariate logistic regression showed an association between mTBI history and ASR (AOR\u2009=\u20091.405; 95% CI\u2009=\u20091.105-1.786, p\u2009<\u20090.01). Adjusting for mechanism of injury (dismounted vs. mounted), LOC and pain, multivariate logistic regression showed an association between mTBI history and ASR (AOR\u2009=\u20091.453; 95% CI\u2009=\u20091.132-1.864, p\u2009<\u20090.01). Prior blast exposure and past psychological health issues were not associated with ASR."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "A history of multiple mTBIs is associated with increased risk of ASR. Future research is warranted."
        }
      ]},
